    "description": "Pastoral nomadism in Iran connects livestock keeping with movement between seasonal grazing areas. Regional communities have combined mobility, settlement, agricultural work, and textile production in different ways, so tribal identity cannot be read directly from residence or occupation.",
    "tags": ["Pastoralism", "Seasonal migration", "Nomadic communities", "Textiles", "Zagros"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:45:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:45:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Nomadic Communities in Iran: Mobility, Livelihoods and Weaving",
      "description": "Understand seasonal pastoral migration in Iran, regional differences among communities, and the relationship between weaving, settlement and identity."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "pastoral-mobility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Movement organized around a livelihood",
        "content": "Pastoral nomadism is organized movement, rather than travel without a destination. Herds and households move between grazing areas as conditions change. In Iran’s mountains, seasonal differences between highland and lower-elevation pastures help explain this system: places unsuitable for year-round cultivation can support livestock at particular times. [s1]\n\nThe household, herd, route, and pasture must be considered together. A blocked route can disrupt access even where grazing survives at either end. Equally, living in a permanent winter house does not necessarily mean that seasonal movement has ended. Ehlers describes combinations of mobile herding, settled residence, agricultural work, and wage employment. Mobility is therefore a variable practice, not a reliable shorthand for an entire community’s identity. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "seasonal-cycle",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The ecological logic of seasonal movement",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Lower-elevation winter grazing\"] -->|\"Seasonal conditions permit departure\"| B[\"Movement toward highland pasture\"]\n  B -->|\"Access to summer grazing\"| C[\"Highland summer pasture\"]\n  C -->|\"Seasonal conditions prompt return\"| D[\"Movement toward winter pasture\"]\n  D -->|\"Access to winter grazing\"| A\n```",
        "summary": "This schematic shows a seasonal grazing cycle. It does not prescribe migration dates, imply that every household travels together, or describe every Iranian pastoral system.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "different-communities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Regional names do not describe identical lives",
        "content": "Museum collections help make regional distinctions visible. The Metropolitan Museum of Art associates Bakhtiari and related Lur migratory ranges with the Zagros Mountains. Its account distinguishes large Bakhtiari pile carpets, mainly woven in settled villages, from portable bags produced by nomadic weavers. Objects sharing a community name can thus belong to different residential and economic settings. [s2]\n\nThe museum places Qashqai communities across an area north and south of Shiraz in southwestern Iran and describes their confederation as predominantly Turkic. Its exhibition account also discusses settlement and changes in weaving traditions. These observations concern the historical and curatorial context of the collection; they should not be converted into a current population count or a claim that all Qashqai households follow the same livelihood. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "weaving-and-knowledge",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Knowledge carried through work",
        "content": "UNESCO’s description of carpet weaving in Fars documents a chain of skills linking animal husbandry to finished textiles. Men shear wool and construct horizontal looms; women spin yarn, select colors, develop designs, and weave. The description emphasizes learning through demonstration and oral instruction within families. Such work makes textiles evidence of practical knowledge and social relationships, as well as artistic production. [s3]\n\nThe designs draw on the weavers’ surroundings and experiences. A portable loom also connects production to the material requirements of mobility. However, this heritage description should not be treated as a complete division of labor for every household, or as proof that skills remain equally secure across communities today. Its value is the documented account of how knowledge is practiced and transmitted. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "adaptation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Adaptation without a single endpoint",
        "content": "Historical scholarship describes pressures from changing land use, pasture regulation, and settlement policies, alongside the adoption of motor transport. These changes altered both movement and livelihoods. They do not justify a prediction that nomadic life must disappear on a fixed timetable. [s1]\n\nUnderstanding a community requires asking separately about identity, residence, herd ownership, movement, and work. The distinction between village carpets and nomadic bags provides a concrete reminder: cultural continuity can coexist with substantial changes in how people live. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
